<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  WHY THE RECEIPT PREVIEW NEEDS THIS, AND WHY IT IS NOT A WEAKENING

  ===========================================================================
  THE SYMPTOM
  ===========================================================================
  On the transfer page the shopper chose `images.jpg`, and instead of a
  thumbnail they got the file NAME — the fallback web/js/proof-preview.js falls
  back to when the browser cannot decode the file. The file was a perfectly
  ordinary JPEG.

  MEASURED on the deployed store rather than guessed. Two facts:

    1. the response carries an ENFORCED `Content-Security-Policy` header (there
       is no `-Report-Only` variant, so Magento's `csp/mode/storefront/
       report_only` has been switched off on this store);
    2. its `img-src` directive reads, in full:
           img-src <third-party hosts…> data: 'self' 'unsafe-inline'
       — `data:` is allowed and `blob:` is NOT.

  `URL.createObjectURL(file)` hands the <img> a `blob:` URL. With `blob:` absent
  from `img-src` the browser refuses to fetch it and fires `error` on the
  element, which is exactly the branch that prints the file name. So the preview
  was working as written and being blocked by policy.

  ===========================================================================
  WHY `blob:` AND NOT A DATA URL
  ===========================================================================
  `FileReader.readAsDataURL()` would sidestep this, because `data:` is already
  allowed. It was rejected: base64 inflates the bytes by a third, and this
  module accepts uploads up to 10 MB (Model\ProofStorage::MAX_BYTES) — so a
  shopper photographing their banking app would have a ~13 MB string built in
  memory to draw a 96px square. An object URL is a handle to bytes the browser
  is already holding and costs nothing.

  ===========================================================================
  IT DOES NOT LOOSEN THE POLICY IN ANY MEANINGFUL SENSE
  ===========================================================================
  `data:` is already on this list, and it is the strictly more dangerous of the
  two: a `data:` URL carries its own arbitrary payload, so injected markup can
  conjure one out of nothing. A `blob:` URL can only ever reference something
  SAME-ORIGIN SCRIPT ALREADY CREATED in this document — there is no way to
  author one from injected markup alone, and it dies with the document. Adding
  it beside `data:` adds no capability an attacker did not already have, and it
  touches `img-src` only: script, frame, connect and form-action are untouched.

  ===========================================================================
  THE FALLBACK STAYS
  ===========================================================================
  This file fixes the store whose CSP Magento generates. It cannot fix one whose
  header is written by a reverse proxy or a CDN, and it will never fix HEIC —
  which this module accepts because it is what an iPhone produces, and which no
  desktop browser decodes. proof-preview.js therefore keeps showing the file
  name whenever the <img> reports `error`, for both of those reasons.
-->
<csp_whitelist xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
               xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="urn:magento:module:Magento_Csp:etc/csp_whitelist.xsd">
    <policies>
        <policy id="img-src">
            <!--
              `type="host"` because that is the only value type the schema has
              besides `hash`, and Magento renders host sources into the
              directive verbatim (Model\Policy\FetchPolicy::getHostSources()).
              A scheme-source is valid in that position per the CSP grammar.
            -->
            <values>
                <value id="spartrak_instapay_object_url" type="host">blob:</value>
            </values>
        </policy>
    </policies>
</csp_whitelist>
